   "l": "Stop up their openings,\nshut their gates,\nblunt their sharp points,",
   "v": true,
   "n": "The four lines from 挫其銳 to 同其塵 are chapter 4's again, all but one character — chapter 4 writes 紛 where this chapter writes 分 (see the next unit) — there said of the Way, here of a man. In both places 其 'its, his, their' has no stated antecedent, and the Chinese does not choose: the sharpness blunted may be the world's or the sage's own. This edition keeps the choice it made at chapter 4 — the world's, 'their' — so that the same lines meet you the same way twice. Wang Bi reads them the other way, of the sage himself: on 和其光 he writes 無所特顯, 'nothing of his is put on show'. Both readings are live, and the phrase 和光同塵 has passed into ordinary Chinese in his sense, of a man who dims his own brilliance and takes his place in the dust with everyone else.\n\nNow read the six lines aloud. 兌 duì chimes with 銳 ruì, and 門 mén, 分 fēn and 塵 chén all rhyme — two thousand years on, in a language the poem never knew, the jingle still holds. It is part of why these lines travelled as a set."
