   "v": true,
   "n": "Here the two texts of this line part company. Chapter 4 prints 紛 fēn, 'a tangle, a snarl'; this chapter prints 分, 'a dividing, a parting-off'. Read it fēn, the splitting, not fèn 'the lot allotted to one' — same graph, two words, and only the sense tells you which. The two characters sound alike, and many editors treat 分 and 紛 as one word written two ways — but 分 has its own edge, and Wang Bi's comment here reads it that way: 除爭原也, 'he removes the root of contention' — and contention begins where things are parted off from one another. We keep the character our text prints and translate that."
