   "l": "This is called the dark sameness.\nSo: he cannot be got at and drawn close,\ncannot be got at and held off;",
   "v": true,
   "i": "This is what is called the dark sameness. He cannot be got at and made a friend of, nor got at and kept at arm's length;",
   "n": "是謂 X is how this book names things — you met it at 是謂玄牝 in chapter 6 — and a phrase the text christens is a word to learn whole, however ordinary its parts look. 玄同 is 'dark sameness': the sameness arrived at by blunting, loosening, dimming and mingling, and dark in this book's sense, the blue-black of water too deep to see into.\n\n不可得而 X now runs six times without a break: 'cannot be got at and then X-ed'. 可 makes what follows passive, so the sage is the one X-ed, never the one X-ing. Six verbs in three pairs of opposites — close and distant, benefit and harm, honour and contempt. The point is not that he is indifferent to them but that there is no handle on him: nothing you can do to him gives you a hold."
  },
