   "i": "And so he is what all under heaven holds precious.",
   "n": "貴 has been a verb for six lines running, 'to honour'; here it is the noun, the thing honoured. Nothing marks the change but its position in the line.\n\nThe turn of the chapter is worth stopping over. Precisely because no one can raise him up or bring him low, he is what everyone prizes — the man with no handle on him is the one nobody can do without.\n\n為 here is the plain verb wéi, 'counts as'. It can also be read as the marker of a passive with its agent standing after it — 'he is prized by all under heaven' — and the line comes out the same either way. Chapter 62 closes with these same five characters."
